  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday morning through next Friday)\nIssued at 234 PM EDT Sat May 30 2026\n\nAn upper trough will usher in a surface front between Monday and \nTuesday from New England. With no real change to our warm, humid \nairmass across the forecast area up until this point, lift along the \nfrontal boundary will further aid in storm development by Monday \nafternoon. Sufficient instability and shear will support the \npotential for isolated wind gusts with any storms that develop in \nnortheast/east Georgia. This is highlighted by a Day 3 Marginal Risk \n(Level 1 of 5) by the Storm Prediction Center. \n\nFollowing this system, the long term ensembles continue to favor the \npotential for a drier weather pattern from mid to late next week. \nUpper ridging draped SW/NE up the MS Valley Region and surface high \npressure extending southward from the Great Lakes to the Southeast \nlooks to remain in place through at least the end of next week. \nTemperatures stay on the warmer side, with forecast highs in the 80s \n(upper 70s at the higher elevations in northeast Georgia) and lows \nin the 50s/60s.",
